I noticed odd foreground_intensities for my dataset.
I have this dataset.json
{
"channel_names": {
"0": "CT" …
Preprocessing gives the following stats:
foreground_intensity_properties_per_channel": {
"0": {
"max": 10623.0, …
When calculating manually I got much more reasonable values (99% -160HU to 100HU, mean ~-40HU). I figured it might be that nnunet was including the ignore class when calculating the statistics. Looking at `DatasetFingerprintExtractor.collect_foreground_intensities` I see `foreground_mask = segmentation[0] > 0`, which I think confirms my suspicion. 
Fix: update foreground mask to properly exclude the ignore class (if present)

seziegler (maintainer): HI @jiwonpoong ,
for multi-modality input you need to define the modalities in your dataset.json, e.g. like this:
 `{ 
 "channel_names": { # formerly modalities 
 "0": "modality 1", 
 "1": "modality 2", 
 "2": "modality 3", 
 "3": "modality 4" 
 }`
See [here](https://github.com/MIC-DKFZ/nnUNet/blob…

@cached_property works like `@property` combined with @lru_cache, but it caches the result only once and it assumes it never changes (the internal state of the class is not modified in the meantime). 
I also assume that the internal state of `LabelManager` and `ConfigurationManager` does not change after creation, please correct me if I'm wrong.
FabianIsensee (maintainer): Hey I would prefer to leave it as is because should I ever decide to fiddle with these classes having @cached_property might cause some really obscure bugs that will be difficult to find

In ischemic stroke segmentation, there are two classes: infarct core and pemnubra, this two regions combine to infarct region. I set ` labels={
 'background': 0,
 'whole infarct': (1, 2),
 'penumbra': (1, ),
 'infarct core': (2, )
 },` but this is not correct. how to fix it , should i set like this : ` labels={
 'background': 0,
 'whole infarct': (1, 2, 3),
 'penumbra': (1, 2),
 'infarct core': (3, )
 },` ?
mrokuss: Hey @GitHub-TXZ 
Region based training allows you to learn areas that are constructed by merging individual labels. In this case you have two labels where the infarct core typically lies inside the pemnubra. Therefore I suggest using this order:
> "labels": {
> "background": 0,
> "penumbra": [1, 2]…
